--- !u!1001 &5958388681621064695
PrefabInstance:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  serializedVersion: 2
  m_Modification:
    serializedVersion: 3
    m_TransformParent: {fileID: 1936824525}
    m_Modifications:
    - target: {fileID: 3657750595127706157, guid: 83f67faf358644b34ad60e68c9dc8f56, type: 3}
      propertyPath: dialogueObjects.Array.size
      value: 4
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 3657750595127706157, guid: 83f67faf358644b34ad60e68c9dc8f56, type: 3}
      propertyPath: dialogueObjects.Array.data[0]._dialogueText
      value: As you may know, polynomials are expression made up of terms that are
        combined using mathematical operations.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 3657750595127706157, guid: 83f67faf358644b34ad60e68c9dc8f56, type: 3}
      propertyPath: dialogueObjects.Array.data[1]._dialogueText
      value: The highest power of the variable in the polynomial determines its degree.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 3657750595127706157, guid: 83f67faf358644b34ad60e68c9dc8f56, type: 3}
      propertyPath: dialogueObjects.Array.data[2]._dialogueText
      value: "As an example, 3x\xB2 + 5x - 2 is a polynomial where, the degree is
        2 because of the x\xB2 term."
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 3657750595127706157, guid: 83f67faf358644b34ad60e68c9dc8f56, type: 3}
      propertyPath: dialogueObjects.Array.data[3]._dialogueText
      value: You can say that a polynomial of degree n will have exactly n root(s)
        within the real or complex plane.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 7782682502209912324, guid: 83f67faf358644b34ad60e68c9dc8f56, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_LocalScale.x
      value: 1
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
